export const booksK = [
  u({ slug: "dune", title: "Dune", author: "Frank Herbert", year: 1965, series: { name: "Dune", slug: "dune", index: 1 }, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Ace", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "The desert planet, the spice and the boy who becomes a messiah. I stamp it fourteen-plus: almost no romance on the page and little bad language, but the villain is written as a predator of boys, the violence includes a child's death, and a drug runs the whole economy.",
    summary: "Paul Atreides is fifteen when his family takes charge of Arrakis, the desert world that produces the spice melange, the most valuable substance in the universe. It is a trap. The Emperor and House Harkonnen betray the Atreides, Paul's father is killed, and Paul and his mother Jessica, a member of the Bene Gesserit sisterhood, escape into the deep desert. They are taken in by the Fremen, the planet's people, who see in Paul a prophecy their own religion was seeded to expect. Paul learns to ride the giant sandworms, takes a Fremen partner, Chani, and drinks the Water of Life, which opens his mind to the futures ahead, including a holy war fought in his name. Herbert writes ecology, religion, politics and power at once, and the book's real argument is a warning about heroes.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up. Many strong readers meet it at twelve or thirteen, and the content itself is not what slows them down: the invented vocabulary and the long political scenes are. What a parent should know: Baron Harkonnen, the villain, is a grotesque whose desire for young boys is stated, not shown, and the book links his cruelty to that desire in a way modern readers rightly question. Violence includes knife duels to the death, the poisoning and murder of Paul's father, a massacre and the off-page killing of Paul's infant son. Romance is a partnership with Chani that produces a child; nothing is described. The spice is an addictive drug, and the plot turns on who controls it. With Dune: Part Three, adapting the sequel Dune Messiah, in theaters December 18, 2026, many families will see the films first. The book is quieter and stranger, and for a fourteen-year-old who likes ideas, it is a good one to argue about.",
    publisherNote: "Ace publishes Dune as adult science fiction. It has been read in high schools for decades, and the films are rated PG-13. I place it a band below adult because nothing is explicit; the weight is in the ideas and the villain.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of six by Frank Herbert. Dune Messiah is short and bleaker; Children of Dune and God Emperor of Dune grow more philosophical and stranger, not more explicit. The later books by Brian Herbert and Kevin J. Anderson are separate and not rated here.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade and critical reviews, the films' MPA ratings as context and consistent reader reporting. I note the Baron specifically because it is what parents most often ask about after the films.",
    sources: [{ label: "Dune: official site", url: "https://dunenovels.com/" }],
    sig: [[2, "A partnership with Chani that produces a child; nothing described. Jessica's concubinage is stated."], [3, "Knife duels, a poisoning, a massacre, the off-page killing of an infant."], [1, "Mild."], [3, "The spice is an addictive drug central to the plot; the Water of Life is a near-fatal trance."], [3, "A villain written as a predator of boys, betrayal, genocide foreseen, religious manipulation."]] }),

  u({ slug: "dune-messiah", title: "Dune Messiah", author: "Frank Herbert", year: 1969, series: { name: "Dune", slug: "dune", index: 2 }, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Ace", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "The short, bleak sequel that Dune: Part Three adapts on December 18, 2026. I stamp it fourteen-plus, like Dune: less fighting and no explicit content, but a holy war of billions, a blinding, a death in childbirth and an ending that walks into the desert.",
    summary: "Twelve years after Dune, Paul Atreides is Emperor, and his Fremen legions have carried a jihad across the known universe in his name, killing billions. Paul sees it all coming and cannot stop it. A conspiracy forms against him: the Bene Gesserit, the Spacing Guild, his own wife-of-state Princess Irulan and a shape-shifting Face Dancer. They bring Paul a gift: a ghola, a living copy of his dead friend Duncan Idaho. Chani, his true partner, cannot conceive, because Irulan has been secretly poisoning her with contraceptives. When Paul is blinded by an atomic stone-burner, he goes on seeing through prescience, until the vision fails him. It is a tragedy, and Herbert meant it as the correction to every reader who cheered Paul on in book one.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up. It is shorter and easier to follow than Dune, but darker. The jihad is reported rather than shown, though the numbers are staggering. A stone-burner blinds a crowd, Paul among them. Chani dies giving birth to twins. A dwarf assassin and a Face Dancer bring menace. Romance is Paul and Chani's long partnership and a cold political marriage; nothing is described. Language is clean. The drug theme continues with spice and with semuta, a drug used by the conspirators. The ending, in which a blind Paul walks into the desert alone as the Fremen custom for the blind requires, is the scene to talk about. For a teen who loved the 2024 film's final shot of Chani walking away, this is the book that explains it.",
    publisherNote: "Ace, adult science fiction. Dune: Part Three is expected to carry the series' PG-13 rating; the book has nothing more explicit than the films.",
    seriesNote: "Book two of six. Children of Dune follows Paul's twins and is similar in weight. Across the original series the band holds at fourteen-plus for me; what rises is philosophical density, not content.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade and critical reviews, the film's release announcement and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Dune: official site", url: "https://dunenovels.com/" }, { label: "Warner Bros.: Dune: Part Three", url: "https://www.warnerbros.com/movies/dune-part-three" }],
    sig: [[1, "A long partnership and a loveless political marriage; nothing described."], [2, "An assassination plot, a stone-burner that blinds, a stabbing. Brief."], [0, "Clean."], [2, "Spice and semuta, a conspirator's drug."], [3, "A holy war of billions, blindness, death in childbirth, a walk into the desert."]] }),

  u({ slug: "a-christmas-carol", title: "A Christmas Carol", author: "Charles Dickens", year: 1843, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Public domain (many editions)", publisherAge: "All ages",
    verdict: "Four ghosts, one miser and the best-known Christmas story in English. I stamp it ten-plus by this site's floor: nothing in it is unsuitable for a younger child, but the Victorian prose and two genuinely eerie ghosts make it a book to read aloud before ten.",
    summary: "Ebenezer Scrooge is a moneylender in London who hates Christmas, underpays his clerk Bob Cratchit and refuses a coin to charity. On Christmas Eve, the ghost of his dead partner Jacob Marley appears, dragging chains forged by his greed, and warns him that three spirits will come. The Ghost of Christmas Past shows Scrooge the lonely boy and the young man who chose money over love. The Ghost of Christmas Present shows the Cratchits' small, joyful dinner and Tiny Tim, who will die if nothing changes, and reveals two starving children called Ignorance and Want. The Ghost of Christmas Yet to Come points silently at a neglected grave. Scrooge wakes on Christmas morning a changed man, and sends the Cratchits the biggest turkey in the shop.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up on this site's scale, and a family read-aloud from about seven. Two moments are worth previewing for a sensitive child: Marley's ghost, whose jaw drops open when he unties his head bandage, and the silent final spirit pointing at Scrooge's grave. Tiny Tim's death is foreseen, not shown, and then undone. The book is short, five 'staves', and a confident reader of ten can manage it alone with some help on the old words. One note for 2026: Ti West's film Ebenezer: A Christmas Carol, starring Johnny Depp, opens in theaters November 13 and is marketed as a dark, horror-leaning take. The book is far gentler than that film is likely to be, so a child who wants the story at Thanksgiving is better off starting with the page or the Muppets.",
    publisherNote: "Public domain, published in countless editions, including illustrated children's versions and abridgements. I rate the full original text.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone. Dickens wrote four more Christmas books, none as well known.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the original text's long publication record, the 2026 film's release announcement and consistent reader and teacher reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Project Gutenberg: A Christmas Carol", url: "https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/46" }],
    sig: [[0, "A broken engagement in Scrooge's past; nothing more."], [1, "Ghosts and a glimpse of Scrooge's grave. Eerie, not violent."], [0, "Clean."], [1, "Christmas punch and a pub mentioned."], [2, "Poverty, a child's foreseen death, loneliness, a man facing his own grave."]] }),

  u({ slug: "the-hobbit", title: "The Hobbit", author: "J.R.R. Tolkien", year: 1937, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "HarperCollins / Houghton Mifflin Harcourt", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "There and back again, with a dragon in the middle. I stamp it ten-plus: one battle, a few deaths and some genuinely creepy spiders, but no romance, no swearing and a narrator who talks to the reader like a grandparent by the fire.",
    summary: "Bilbo Baggins is a comfortable hobbit who likes second breakfasts and dislikes adventures. The wizard Gandalf volunteers him as burglar for thirteen dwarves led by Thorin Oakenshield, who mean to reclaim their mountain home and its treasure from the dragon Smaug. On the road, Bilbo is nearly eaten by trolls, captured by goblins, and in the dark under the mountains he wins a riddle game against Gollum and pockets a magic ring. There are giant spiders in Mirkwood, a prison of wood-elves, a barrel escape down the river and a conversation with Smaug himself. The dragon's death brings armies to the mountain, and Thorin's greed for the treasure nearly starts a war among friends before a goblin army forces them together.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up, and one of the great read-alouds for children from about seven. The scariest scenes for a young listener are the Mirkwood spiders, which wrap the dwarves in webs to eat later, and Gollum in the dark. The Battle of Five Armies is told briefly and largely from a distance after Bilbo is knocked out. Thorin and two young dwarves die after the battle, and Thorin's deathbed reconciliation with Bilbo is sad and well handled. Characters smoke pipes and the wood-elves get drunk on wine, which leads to the barrel escape. No romance at all. Searches for 'is The Hobbit appropriate for an eight-year-old' are asking about the book, and my answer is yes, read aloud; the Peter Jackson films are much more violent than the book and are a separate decision.",
    publisherNote: "HarperCollins in the UK and Houghton Mifflin Harcourt (now HarperCollins) in the US publish it for children and adults alike. Content sits comfortably in middle grade.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone prelude to The Lord of the Rings, which is longer, slower and darker, with more battle violence. I would rate The Fellowship of the Ring at twelve-plus. The Hobbit itself is the right first step.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, long-standing critical and teacher consensus and consistent parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "The Tolkien Estate", url: "https://www.tolkienestate.com/" }],
    sig: [[0, "None."], [2, "A battle, three deaths after it, spiders and goblins. Not gory."], [0, "Clean."], [2, "Pipe-smoking throughout; wood-elves drunk on wine."], [1, "Greed and its cost; the death of a friend."]] }),

  u({ slug: "harry-potter-and-the-prisoner-of-azkaban", title: "Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban", author: "J.K. Rowling", year: 1999, series: { name: "Harry Potter", slug: "harry-potter", index: 3 }, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Scholastic (US)", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "The one with the Dementors, a werewolf teacher and a time-turner. I keep it at ten-plus, the last Harry Potter book I would put there: nobody dies, but the Dementors are the series' most frightening idea, and the backstory of Harry's parents' betrayal lands hard.",
    summary: "Harry's third year opens with an escape: Sirius Black, the first prisoner ever to break out of the wizard prison Azkaban, is said to be hunting Harry. Hogwarts is guarded by Dementors, faceless prison guards who drain every happy feeling from anyone nearby, and Harry, who remembers his mother's last scream when they come close, faints. The new Defence teacher, Remus Lupin, teaches him the Patronus charm. Hermione is everywhere at once, Hagrid's hippogriff Buckbeak is sentenced to death, and Ron's rat Scabbers is not what he seems. On the night of the full moon, everything Harry thought he knew about his parents' deaths turns upside down.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up. This is the best book in the series for many readers and the right one for a ten-year-old who loved the first two. The Dementors are the thing to know about: J.K. Rowling has said they were drawn from her own experience of depression, and they feel like it. Harry hears his mother's murder when they come near. A werewolf transformation is frightening but no one is badly hurt. Buckbeak's execution is threatened and then avoided. Language has a few uses of 'bloody'. Butterbeer and a pub in Hogsmeade are the only substances. The heavier note is betrayal: the person who sold Harry's parents to Voldemort was a friend. HBO's series begins with book one on December 25, 2026, and this is where many families will pause; after this, the series moves up a band.",
    publisherNote: "Scholastic in the US, middle grade. Content matches.",
    seriesNote: "Book three of seven. Goblet of Fire, book four, brings the first on-page murder of a classmate, which is why I move it to twelve-plus. From book five onward the series sits higher still.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the author's public comments on the Dementors and long-standing consistent reader and parent reporting.",
    sig: [[0, "None; Ron and Hermione bicker."], [2, "Dementor attacks, a werewolf, an execution threatened. No deaths."], [1, "A few uses of 'bloody'."], [1, "Butterbeer and a village pub."], [2, "A parent's murder relived, betrayal by a friend, wrongful imprisonment, despair embodied."]] }),

  u({ slug: "harry-potter-and-the-goblet-of-fire", title: "Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire", author: "J.K. Rowling", year: 2000, series: { name: "Harry Potter", slug: "harry-potter", index: 4 }, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Scholastic (US)", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "The Triwizard Tournament, the Yule Ball and the return of Voldemort. I move it up to twelve-plus: a student is murdered on the page, and the graveyard scene is the series' turning point from adventure to war.",
    summary: "Hogwarts hosts the Triwizard Tournament, a contest between three European wizarding schools, and the Goblet of Fire unexpectedly chooses a fourth champion: fourteen-year-old Harry, who is too young and did not enter. Ron believes he cheated. The tasks are a dragon, a rescue from the bottom of the lake and a hedge maze full of monsters. In between come the Quidditch World Cup and the Death Eater riot that follows it, and at Christmas the Yule Ball, where Harry and Ron fumble their invitations and Hermione arrives with Viktor Krum. The final task is a trap. Harry and Cedric Diggory grab the cup together and are carried to a graveyard, where Cedric is killed and Voldemort is reborn.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up from me, with the honest note that many ten- and eleven-year-olds read straight through and cope. The step up is the graveyard: Cedric is killed with one spell, Harry is tied to a headstone and cut for his blood, and Wormtail cuts off his own hand to complete the ritual. It is short but vivid. The Death Eaters' torment of a Muggle family at the World Cup is the other dark scene. Romance arrives: crushes, the Yule Ball, Ron's jealousy over Hermione, which is all played for comedy and awkwardness. A house-elf gets drunk on butterbeer. The Yule Ball makes it the Christmas book of the series, and it ends with a father weeping over his son. For a ten-year-old, read the last hundred pages together.",
    publisherNote: "Scholastic in the US, middle grade. The 2005 film was rated PG-13 for the same graveyard scene; I follow that line.",
    seriesNote: "Book four of seven, and the turn. Order of the Phoenix is longer and angrier; Half-Blood Prince and Deathly Hallows are war books. I rate the later volumes at twelve-plus and above.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the film's MPA rating as context and long-standing consistent reader and parent reporting.",
    sig: [[1, "The Yule Ball, crushes, jealousy and one kiss off the page."], [3, "A student murdered, a blood ritual, a self-amputation, a riot."], [1, "Mild; a few 'bloody's."], [1, "A house-elf drunk on butterbeer."], [2, "The murder of a classmate, a grieving father, prejudice against house-elves and giants."]] }),

  u({ slug: "percy-jackson-the-battle-of-the-labyrinth", title: "The Battle of the Labyrinth", author: "Rick Riordan", year: 2008, series: { name: "Percy Jackson and the Olympians", slug: "percy-jackson-and-the-olympians", index: 4 }, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Disney-Hyperion", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "The fourth Percy Jackson book: a maze under America, an inventor who never died and a war at camp. I keep it at ten-plus: deaths rise and the first kiss arrives, but the jokes and the bloodless monster fights hold.",
    summary: "Percy's freshman orientation is interrupted by cheerleaders who turn out to be empousai, vampire-demons. Back at Camp Half-Blood, he and Annabeth discover an entrance to Daedalus's Labyrinth, a living maze that spreads under the whole country, and Luke's army means to use it to invade camp. Annabeth leads her first quest, with Percy, Grover and Tyson, to find Daedalus before Luke does. They meet a three-headed rancher, Hephaestus, a Sphinx who asks multiple-choice questions, Calypso on her island, and Pan, the god Grover has searched for all his life. The book ends with a full battle at camp, and with Luke's body becoming a vessel for the Titan lord Kronos.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up. The new things: a first kiss between Percy and Annabeth, quickly interrupted, and a crush on Calypso that the series treats sweetly. Rachel Elizabeth Dare, a mortal girl who can see through the Mist, adds a love-triangle shade without any heat. Violence is bigger: the battle at camp has deaths among the half-bloods, and Daedalus dies by choice. Pan's fading is a gentle, sad scene about letting go. Kronos rising in Luke's body is the creepiest image in the series so far. Still clean, still bloodless, still funny. Season 3 of the Disney+ show, adapting The Titan's Curse, premieres November 20, 2026, and this is the next book for a reader who catches up on the show and wants to stay ahead.",
    publisherNote: "Disney-Hyperion, middle grade. Content matches.",
    seriesNote: "Book four of five. The Last Olympian is a war book with the series' highest death count, still in the same band for me. Drift across the whole series is minimal.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent parent and reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Rick Riordan: Percy Jackson series", url: "https://rickriordan.com/series/percy-jackson/" }],
    sig: [[1, "A first kiss, interrupted; a crush on Calypso."], [2, "A battle at camp with deaths; a hero's death by choice."], [0, "Clean."], [0, "None."], [1, "A god fading away, a friend possessed, a parent's absence."]] }),

  u({ slug: "a-good-girls-guide-to-murder", title: "A Good Girl's Guide to Murder", author: "Holly Jackson", year: 2019, series: { name: "A Good Girl's Guide to Murder", slug: "a-good-girls-guide-to-murder", index: 1 }, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Delacorte Press", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "A school project reopens a closed murder case. I stamp it fourteen-plus: no sex on the page, but a drug-facilitated rape is the key to the mystery, a dog is killed, and the teen party drinking and drug dealing are part of the investigation.",
    summary: "Five years ago in Little Kilton, a quiet English town, seventeen-year-old Andie Bell disappeared, and her boyfriend Sal Singh was found dead in the woods, apparently by suicide, apparently guilty. Pip Fitz-Amobi, a high-achieving senior, chooses the case for her final-year project, convinced Sal was innocent. With Sal's younger brother Ravi, she interviews classmates, reads Andie's texts and follows a trail through a drug dealer, a secret older boyfriend and a party scene that the town would rather not discuss. The investigation is told partly in her project logs, with transcripts and maps, and someone in town starts leaving her warnings. The solution has two layers, and both are darker than a school project should be.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up. Thirteen-year-olds ask for it by name, so here is what a parent is weighing. Romance is a slow, sweet partnership with Ravi; a kiss at most. The heavy material is in the mystery: a character drugged girls at parties and raped them, revealed late and not described in detail. Pip is threatened, and her family's dog is killed as a warning, which upsets many readers more than anything else in the book. Teen drinking and drug dealing are on the page. Language is British and occasionally strong. Suicide is discussed. The Netflix and BBC series from 2024 is rated in the same range. A twelve-year-old who loved The Inheritance Games will want this, and it is a real step up.",
    publisherNote: "Delacorte Press (Penguin Random House) publishes it as young adult in the US; Electric Monkey in the UK. Content sits at the upper end of YA.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of a trilogy, plus a prequel novella, Kill Joy. Good Girl, Bad Blood stays in the same band; As Good As Dead is much darker, with a stalker plot and Pip's own act of violence, and I would rate it sixteen-plus. That is the drift to watch.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the adaptation's ratings as context and consistent reader and parent reporting, including the widely discussed reveal.",
    sources: [{ label: "Holly Jackson: official site", url: "https://www.hojay.co.uk/" }],
    sig: [[1, "A slow partnership; a kiss at most."], [2, "A murder, threats, a dog killed as a warning."], [2, "Occasional strong language."], [3, "Teen drinking, drug dealing and drugged drinks are central to the case."], [3, "Drug-facilitated rape revealed; suicide; a town's prejudice."]] }),

  u({ slug: "red-rising", title: "Red Rising", author: "Pierce Brown", year: 2014, series: { name: "Red Rising Saga", slug: "red-rising-saga", index: 1 }, ageBand: "16-18", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Del Rey", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "The Hunger Games with Roman legions on Mars, written for adults. I stamp it sixteen-plus. To answer the search directly: it is not spicy, but rape at the Institute is part of the plot, and the violence is the most brutal on this shelf below eighteen.",
    summary: "Darrow is a Red, a miner deep under the surface of Mars, told his people are terraforming the planet for future generations. His wife Eo is hanged for singing a forbidden song, and Darrow learns the truth: the surface was colonised centuries ago, and the Reds are slaves to the Golds, a ruling caste bred for beauty and war. A rebel group rebuilds his body into a Gold and sends him to the Institute, a brutal academy where young Golds are divided into houses and fight a war game for control of a valley. There is no rule against killing, and the students learn that quickly. Darrow survives by becoming a leader, by winning the loyalty of a sharp Gold girl called Mustang, and by becoming the thing he was sent to destroy.",
    ripeFor: "Sixteen and up from me, and it is one of the most-asked 'is it appropriate' books for thirteen-year-olds who loved The Hunger Games. The answer on heat is easy: Darrow's marriage and his bond with Mustang are passionate but not explicit. The harder parts are elsewhere. Students at the Institute kill each other with swords and bare hands through a starving winter, and some of them rape captured girls; the attacks are not described in detail, but they are part of the plot, and Darrow intervenes in one. Language is mostly invented ('gorydamn', 'bloodydamn') with some real swearing. A fifteen-year-old who has read Mockingjay will manage it; for a thirteen-year-old I would suggest Scythe first.",
    publisherNote: "Del Rey publishes the series as adult science fiction. It was not written as YA, and the Institute sections are why.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of the saga. Golden Son and Morning Star, which complete the first trilogy, are war books with more death. The second trilogy, from Iron Gold onward, adds multiple points of view, including more sexual violence and torture. I would rate the later books eighteen-plus. Drift is significant.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent reader reporting, including widely shared content warnings.",
    sources: [{ label: "Pierce Brown: Red Rising", url: "https://www.piercebrown.com/" }],
    sig: [[2, "A passionate marriage and a slow-building bond; nothing explicit."], [4, "Sustained, brutal killing among students, a hanging, a starving winter."], [2, "Mostly invented curses, with some real ones."], [2, "Drinking among Golds."], [3, "Rape at the Institute, slavery, a spouse's execution."]] }),

  u({ slug: "mistborn-the-final-empire", title: "Mistborn: The Final Empire", author: "Brandon Sanderson", year: 2006, series: { name: "Mistborn", slug: "mistborn", index: 1 }, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Tor", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "A heist against an immortal god-king, with magic you swallow. I stamp it twelve-plus: adult-published but written clean, with no sex and little swearing. The violence is real, and the empire's abuse of its enslaved people, including its women, is stated plainly.",
    summary: "For a thousand years ash has fallen from the sky over the Final Empire, and the Lord Ruler, an immortal emperor, has kept the skaa, the enslaved working class, in their place. Kelsier, a charismatic thief and the only survivor of the Pits of Hathsin, gathers a crew for the ultimate job: overthrow the Lord Ruler. He recruits Vin, a street urchin who does not know she is Mistborn, someone who can swallow metals and 'burn' them for powers: pushing and pulling on metal, heightened senses, strength, the ability to soothe or inflame emotions. Vin goes undercover as a noblewoman at the court's balls, falls for a noble heir called Elend, and learns to trust a crew for the first time. The ending is a series of reversals that Sanderson fans still argue about.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up. Brandon Sanderson writes without sex or strong language, and this is the adult fantasy I most often recommend for a strong twelve-year-old ready to move beyond YA. Romance between Vin and Elend is a courtship at balls; a kiss. The violence is the thing to know about: fights with metal coins and daggers kill many people, Kelsier's revenge is bloody, and there are public executions. The book states that nobles are permitted to sleep with skaa women and must then kill them; nothing is shown, but it is the clearest moment where the book's adult shelf shows. Vin was beaten by her brother and trusts no one, which is the emotional core. Long, but fast.",
    publisherNote: "Tor publishes it as adult fantasy. Sanderson's books are widely read by teens, and his publisher shelves them for adults mainly because of length and style, not content.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of the original trilogy. The Well of Ascension and The Hero of Ages hold the same band, with more war. The later Wax and Wayne books are lighter in tone. Drift is minimal.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Brandon Sanderson: Mistborn", url: "https://www.brandonsanderson.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "A courtship at balls and a kiss."], [3, "Battles, executions, revenge killings. Some blood."], [1, "Mild; invented curses."], [1, "Drinking at court; the magic is swallowed metal, not a drug."], [3, "Enslavement, sexual exploitation stated, a girl abused by her brother."]] }),

  u({ slug: "project-hail-mary", title: "Project Hail Mary", author: "Andy Weir", year: 2021, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Ballantine Books", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "A science teacher wakes up alone in space and makes a friend. I stamp it twelve-plus: no romance and almost no violence; the swearing is the main reason it is not lower, and the joy of the science is why it is one of the easiest adult books to give a teen.",
    summary: "Ryland Grace wakes up on a spaceship with no memory of who he is and two crewmates dead in the bunks beside him. Memories return in flashbacks: he was a middle-school science teacher, and Earth's sun is dimming because of a microorganism eating its energy. Grace is on a one-way mission to Tau Ceti, the only nearby star that seems immune, to find out why. He is not alone in the system. Another ship from another world is there for the same reason, with a spider-like engineer aboard whom Grace names Rocky. They cannot breathe the same air or speak the same language, and the friendship they build, through music, maths and a great deal of improvised engineering, is the heart of the book.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up, and a superb gift for a teen who likes science, puzzles or The Martian's sense of humour. There is no romance at all. Violence is minimal: two crew members are dead before the book starts and a later accident injures Grace badly. Language is the only real caution: Grace swears, including some uses of the f-word, more often than a children's book would. The heavier idea, revealed late, is that Grace did not volunteer for the mission, and how that came about is a good discussion for a teen. The 2026 film with Ryan Gosling, rated PG-13, came out in March, which makes this a natural pairing over the holidays.",
    publisherNote: "Ballantine Books, adult. Content is far milder than most adult fiction, which is why teachers and librarians hand it to teens.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the film's MPA rating as context and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Andy Weir: official site", url: "https://www.andyweirauthor.com/" }],
    sig: [[0, "None."], [1, "Dead crewmates and a serious accident; nothing graphic."], [2, "Occasional strong language, including the f-word."], [0, "None to speak of."], [2, "An unwilling sacrifice, isolation, the threat of extinction."]] }),

  u({ slug: "the-giver", title: "The Giver", author: "Lois Lowry", year: 1993, series: { name: "The Giver Quartet", slug: "the-giver-quartet", index: 1 }, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "HMH Books for Young Readers", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "The perfect community and the memory it erased. I stamp it twelve-plus: there is almost nothing on the surface, but the scene where Jonas watches his father 'release' a newborn is one of the most disturbing moments in middle-grade fiction, and it needs a grown-up nearby.",
    summary: "Jonas lives in a community without pain, hunger, war or colour, where every family has two children assigned to it, every job is chosen by the Elders, and everyone takes a daily pill once 'Stirrings' begin. At the Ceremony of Twelve, Jonas is selected as the next Receiver of Memory, and an old man called the Giver begins transmitting the memories of the world before: snow, sunshine, colour, love, and also war, starvation and grief. Jonas learns what 'release' really means. When the community decides that a baby his family has been caring for will be released, Jonas makes a decision, and the book ends in the snow with an ending that readers still debate.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up from me, and it is taught widely in sixth and seventh grade. A ten- or eleven-year-old can read it, and plenty do for school, but I would want to read the release scene with them. Jonas watches a recording of his father injecting a newborn twin, cheerfully, and putting the small body in a chute. It is not gory, and that is exactly why it lands. A memory of war shows a dying soldier. 'Stirrings', the first sexual feelings, are mentioned and suppressed with a pill. There is nothing else to worry about: no swearing, no romance beyond a dream about a friend. It is short, and it is one of the best books for teaching a child to question a perfect-sounding system.",
    publisherNote: "HMH Books for Young Readers publishes it as middle grade, for ages twelve and up. It won the 1994 Newbery Medal and is one of the most frequently challenged books in American schools.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of a quartet. Gathering Blue, Messenger and Son are separate stories that converge, with similar content. Drift is minimal.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, the Newbery record, American Library Association challenge data and consistent teacher and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Lois Lowry: official site", url: "https://loislowry.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "'Stirrings' suppressed with a pill; a dream about a friend."], [3, "An infant killed by injection; a memory of a soldier's death."], [0, "Clean."], [1, "A daily pill to suppress feelings."], [3, "Infanticide and euthanasia as policy, emotional suppression, isolation."]] }),

  u({ slug: "heir-of-fire", title: "Heir of Fire", author: "Sarah J. Maas", year: 2014, series: { name: "Throne of Glass", slug: "throne-of-glass", index: 3 }, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Bloomsbury", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "Celaena's grief book, and where the witches arrive. I stamp it fourteen-plus, like Crown of Midnight. To answer the search: it is not spicy; the heat is low. The violence and the witches' cruelty are what rise.",
    summary: "Grieving and drunk in the kingdom of Wendlyn, Celaena Sardothien is found by Rowan Whitethorn, a fae warrior who takes her to his queen, Maeve, her aunt. Maeve will answer her questions about the Wyrdkeys only if Celaena masters her fire magic and her own identity as Aelin Galathynius, heir to the throne of Terrasen. Rowan trains her, brutally, and the two become something like family. In Adarlan, Chaol and Dorian face the king's growing power and the Valg demons behind it, and Dorian falls for a healer, Sorscha. In the mountains, Manon Blackbeak, heir to a clan of iron-toothed witches, trains to ride wyverns for the king's army, and the book's most chilling scenes belong to her.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up. The romance is low: Dorian and Sorscha sleep together, which is described briefly and gently, and Celaena and Rowan's bond is not yet romantic. Violence rises sharply. Manon's witches kill without thought, and she is ordered to sacrifice a captured witch from an enemy clan, which she does. The Valg demons possess people. A character readers love is executed in front of Dorian. Language is moderate. The emotional story is Celaena's grief, drinking and self-hatred and her slow recovery, which is handled well. For a teen who has read the first two books, this is the last one I would approve without a conversation; Queen of Shadows is the next step up.",
    publisherNote: "Bloomsbury, young adult. Bloomsbury has kept the series in teen sections, though the later books are much more adult.",
    seriesNote: "Book three of eight. Queen of Shadows (four) is where I move the series to sixteen-plus, and Empire of Storms (five) is where explicit sex begins. This is the last book before the steep part of the climb.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Sarah J. Maas: official site", url: "https://sarahjmaas.com/" }],
    sig: [[2, "One brief, gentle sex scene between secondary characters."], [3, "Witch rites, a sacrifice, demon possession, an execution."], [2, "Occasional strong language."], [2, "Celaena drinks to numb grief."], [3, "Grief, self-hatred, demon possession, a forced killing."]] }),

  u({ slug: "queen-of-shadows", title: "Queen of Shadows", author: "Sarah J. Maas", year: 2015, series: { name: "Throne of Glass", slug: "throne-of-glass", index: 4 }, ageBand: "16-18", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Bloomsbury", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "Aelin comes home to settle her debts. I move the series up to sixteen-plus here: the sex is still behind the door, but the heat rises on the page, the violence is the series' most brutal yet, and the backstory of child sexual exploitation is spelled out.",
    summary: "Aelin Galathynius, no longer calling herself Celaena, returns to Rifthold to take down two men: Arobynn Hamel, the King of Assassins who raised and abused her, and the king of Adarlan, who has enslaved his own son Dorian to a Valg demon. She allies with Lysandra, a courtesan who grew up in Arobynn's world, and with Aedion, her cousin, a general held for execution. Rowan arrives from Wendlyn, and their bond turns into romance. Manon Blackbeak, in the witches' camp, discovers what the king is doing to the Yellowlegs witches and begins to rebel. It is a long revenge story, and it ends with the glass castle shattered.",
    ripeFor: "Sixteen and up from me. Romance between Aelin and Rowan is on the page and physical, with heavy kissing and touching; the sex itself is not shown here. The violence is the sharpest in the series so far: Arobynn's death, torture by the Valg, and Lysandra's shape-shifting fights. The heaviest note is backstory. Lysandra was sold as a child to a brothel, and the book makes plain what that meant. Arobynn was a manipulative, violent guardian to Aelin. And the witches' camp holds the most disturbing idea in the series: the king is using witches to breed demon offspring. For a fourteen-year-old who loved Heir of Fire, this is the volume I would preview first.",
    publisherNote: "Bloomsbury, young adult, though the content here moves out of the YA middle. The publisher has not reclassified the series as it did ACOTAR.",
    seriesNote: "Book four of eight. Empire of Storms (five) and Kingdom of Ash (eight) contain explicit sex scenes; I rate them eighteen-plus. Tower of Dawn (six) is gentler. Drift is steep from here.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Sarah J. Maas: official site", url: "https://sarahjmaas.com/" }],
    sig: [[3, "Physical romance on the page; sex not shown."], [4, "Torture, revenge killings, demon possession."], [2, "Occasional strong language."], [2, "Drinking."], [3, "Child sexual exploitation in backstory, an abusive guardian, forced breeding of witches."]] }),

  u({ slug: "warriors-into-the-wild", title: "Warriors: Into the Wild", author: "Erin Hunter", year: 2003, series: { name: "Warriors", slug: "warriors", index: 1 }, ageBand: "10-12", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "HarperCollins", publisherAge: "Middle grade",
    verdict: "A house cat joins a clan of wild warriors in the forest. I stamp it ten-plus: there is no romance or swearing, but cats fight to the death, and a senior warrior turns out to be a murderer. Warrior Cats is gentler than the battle scenes suggest and darker than the covers.",
    summary: "Rusty is a kittypet, a house cat, who dreams of the forest beyond his garden fence. One night he ventures in and meets a young cat from ThunderClan, one of four wild clans that share the forest and follow the warrior code. Invited to join, he becomes Firepaw, an apprentice, and has to prove himself against clan cats who sneer at his soft origins. He makes friends with Graypaw, the nervous Ravenpaw and the medicine cat Spottedleaf, takes in Yellowfang, an exiled medicine cat from ShadowClan, and grows suspicious of Tigerclaw, a powerful warrior whose story of a battle does not match what Ravenpaw saw. Clan battles and a stolen litter of kits build to Firepaw earning his warrior name.",
    ripeFor: "Ten and up, and most fans start at eight or nine, which is fine for a reader who handles some death. The fights between clans have claws and blood, and several named cats die, including the gentle Spottedleaf, killed by a cat from a rival clan. Tigerclaw is revealed as a murderer who wants power. There are no human-style romances in book one, no swearing and no substances. The series is very long, more than eighty books across many arcs, and stays in roughly the same band. For a cat-loving child, it is one of the most reliable ways to turn a reluctant reader into a series reader.",
    publisherNote: "HarperCollins publishes the series as middle grade, ages eight to twelve. Content matches.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of the first arc of six, with dozens more books, novellas and manga. Later arcs bring kits, mates and more deaths; none leaves the band for me. Drift is minimal.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent parent and reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Warriors: official site", url: "https://www.warriorcats.com/" }],
    sig: [[0, "None in book one."], [2, "Clan battles with claws and blood; several named cats die."], [0, "Clean."], [0, "None."], [1, "A murderous warrior, loyalty and belonging, grief."]] }),

  u({ slug: "let-it-snow", title: "Let It Snow: Three Holiday Romances", author: "John Green, Maureen Johnson and Lauren Myracle", year: 2008, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Speak (Penguin)", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "Three linked teen romances in one Christmas Eve blizzard. I stamp it twelve-plus: kisses, a snowbound Waffle House, cheerleaders and a teacup pig, with mild language and almost nothing else. The answer to 'clean Christmas books for teens'.",
    summary: "A blizzard shuts down the small town of Gracetown on Christmas Eve. In Maureen Johnson's story, Jubilee's train strands her on the way to her grandparents, after her parents are arrested in a riot over a collectable Christmas village, and she walks through the snow to the house of a boy called Stuart. In John Green's, Tobin and his friends race across town through the drifts to a Waffle House full of stranded cheerleaders, and Tobin realises he likes his best friend, the Duke. In Lauren Myracle's, Addie, who cheated on her boyfriend and is miserable about it, has to collect a teacup pig for her friend and learns to stop making everything about herself. The three stories overlap and end in the same place.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up, and a good pick for an eleven-year-old who likes romantic comedy. Romance is kisses and talk about kisses. Addie's storyline is about a kiss with another boy and the fallout, handled as a lesson in self-centredness rather than anything risqué. Language is mild, with a handful of stronger words. There is no drinking to speak of, and the violence is a car stuck in a snowdrift. The 2019 Netflix film changes the stories considerably and is rated TV-PG. As a gift for a teen who wants a Christmas read without the bedroom door, this is the safest bet on the site.",
    publisherNote: "Speak, Penguin's YA paperback imprint. Content sits young for YA.",
    seriesNote: "A standalone anthology.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Penguin Random House: Let It Snow", url: "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/301011/let-it-snow-by-john-green-maureen-johnson-and-lauren-myracle/" }],
    sig: [[1, "Kisses and crushes."], [0, "None."], [1, "Mild, with a few stronger words."], [0, "None to speak of."], [1, "Guilt, a breakup, parents in jail over a Christmas village."]] }),

  u({ slug: "dash-and-lilys-book-of-dares", title: "Dash & Lily's Book of Dares", author: "Rachel Cohn and David Levithan", year: 2010, series: { name: "Dash & Lily", slug: "dash-and-lily", index: 1 }, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Knopf Books for Young Readers", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "A red notebook of dares left on a shelf at the Strand, passed back and forth across New York at Christmas. I stamp it fourteen-plus: the romance is sweet and the heat is low, but a party with underage drinking and some strong language put it a step above Let It Snow.",
    summary: "Lily is sixteen, loves Christmas, and has been left alone in Manhattan for the holidays by her parents and her brother. Her brother's idea is a red notebook full of clues, hidden among the books at the Strand, for the right boy to find. Dash, a cynical book-lover who hates Christmas and has lied to both his divorced parents so he can spend the holidays alone, finds it. They pass the notebook back and forth, daring each other into odd corners of New York in the week before Christmas. They do not meet for most of the book, and when they do, it is not what either imagined.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up from me, though many thirteen-year-olds read it happily. Romance is a slow build to kisses. The step up from a younger Christmas book is a party scene with underage drinking and some strong language, including the f-word, which comes mostly from Dash and is used for character, not shock. Dash's sarcasm about his family hides some sadness about his parents' divorce. The 2020 Netflix series is rated TV-14. For a teen who likes bookshops, banter and New York at Christmas, it is a lovely gift.",
    publisherNote: "Knopf Books for Young Readers publishes it as young adult. Content matches.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of three. The Twelve Days of Dash and Lily and Mind the Gap, Dash and Lily are similar in tone and stay in the same band.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the series' TV rating as context and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Penguin Random House: Dash & Lily's Book of Dares", url: "https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/90398/dash-and-lilys-book-of-dares-by-rachel-cohn-and-david-levithan/" }],
    sig: [[1, "A slow build to kisses."], [1, "A minor injury, played lightly."], [2, "Occasional strong language, mostly Dash's."], [2, "Underage drinking at a party."], [1, "Divorce, loneliness at the holidays."]] }),

  u({ slug: "the-summer-i-turned-pretty", title: "The Summer I Turned Pretty", author: "Jenny Han", year: 2009, series: { name: "The Summer I Turned Pretty", slug: "the-summer-i-turned-pretty", index: 1 }, ageBand: "12-14", grade: "middle-school", publisher: "Simon & Schuster Books for Young Readers", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "A beach house, two brothers and the summer a girl turns sixteen. I stamp book one twelve-plus: kisses, one party with beer and a mother's illness. The series grows up fast, and the Prime Video show is older than book one, so check which a reader means.",
    summary: "Every summer, Isabel 'Belly' Conklin goes with her mother and brother to Cousins Beach, where they share a house with her mother's best friend, Susannah, and Susannah's sons, Conrad and Jeremiah. Belly has loved Conrad, the brooding older one, for as long as she can remember. The summer she turns sixteen, everyone notices she has grown up: a new boy, Cam, likes her, Jeremiah starts to, and Conrad is acting strangely. Flashbacks to earlier summers show the friendships that built the triangle. The secret everyone is keeping, which explains Conrad and the adults' sadness, is Susannah's cancer, which has come back.",
    ripeFor: "Twelve and up for book one. Romance is crushes, a first kiss with Cam and kisses with the Fisher boys; nothing more. There is a beach party where teens drink beer, and Conrad drinks and smokes. Language is mild. The weight is Susannah's illness and the way the children learn about it, which is handled tenderly. The thing to know before saying yes is drift and the show. It's Not Summer Without You (book two) is about grief after Susannah's death and includes more drinking; We'll Always Have Summer (book three) is about an engagement at nineteen and a night together implied. The Prime Video series is rated TV-14 and includes more mature content than book one.",
    publisherNote: "Simon & Schuster Books for Young Readers, young adult. Book one sits at the young end of YA.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of a trilogy. Books two and three rise to fourteen-plus for me, mainly for grief, drinking and an implied sexual relationship in book three.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews, the show's TV rating as context and consistent reader and parent reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Jenny Han: official site", url: "https://www.dearjennyhan.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "Crushes and first kisses."], [0, "None."], [1, "Mild."], [2, "Teens drink beer at a party; one character smokes."], [2, "A parent's cancer, kept secret, and divorce."]] }),

  u({ slug: "the-name-of-the-wind", title: "The Name of the Wind", author: "Patrick Rothfuss", year: 2007, series: { name: "The Kingkiller Chronicle", slug: "the-kingkiller-chronicle", index: 1 }, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "DAW Books", publisherAge: "Adult",
    verdict: "A legend tells his own story in an inn at the end of the world. I stamp it fourteen-plus: no sex and little swearing, but a massacre, years of homelessness as a child and a drug addiction plot sit inside a slow, beautiful book. The sequel climbs higher.",
    summary: "Kote is an innkeeper in a quiet village. He is also Kvothe, the most notorious wizard, musician and killer of his age, now hiding. When a travelling scribe recognises him, Kvothe agrees to tell his true story over three days. Day one, this book, covers his childhood with a troupe of travelling players, the slaughter of his family by the mysterious Chandrian, three years living alone on the streets of the city of Tarbean, and his admission at fifteen to the University, where he studies sympathy, a magic of links and energy. There he makes a rival of a rich student, Ambrose, falls for a mysterious girl called Denna, and goes after a dragon-like draccus addicted to a drug. The frame story hints at a war coming.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up, and often asked about for teens who love Mistborn. Romance is Kvothe's long, unspoken love for Denna: talk and walks, no sex in this book. The violence that matters is early: Kvothe finds his parents and the troupe murdered, and then survives Tarbean, where he is beaten and robbed, and witnesses older boys attacking a younger child, with sexual assault implied. Language is mild. Substances matter: denner resin is an addictive drug, a draccus is crazed by it and a character is addicted, and Kvothe drinks. The book is long and slow and rewards patience. The note for parents is the sequel: The Wise Man's Fear includes a long sexual episode with a fae temptress and I rate it sixteen-plus. The third book has not been published.",
    publisherNote: "DAW Books, adult fantasy. Content is milder than many adult fantasies, which is why it is a common crossover pick.",
    seriesNote: "Book one of a planned trilogy. The Wise Man's Fear (book two) adds significant sexual content and moves up a band. The concluding book has no release date.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Patrick Rothfuss: official site", url: "https://www.patrickrothfuss.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "A long, chaste longing for Denna."], [3, "A massacre of a family, street violence against a child, a fight with a draccus."], [1, "Mild."], [3, "Denner resin addiction on the page; drinking."], [3, "A child orphaned and homeless for years, an implied predatory threat."]] }),

  u({ slug: "crooked-kingdom", title: "Crooked Kingdom", author: "Leigh Bardugo", year: 2016, series: { name: "Six of Crows", slug: "six-of-crows", index: 2 }, ageBand: "14-16", grade: "high-school", publisher: "Henry Holt", publisherAge: "Young adult",
    verdict: "The Crows come home to Ketterdam to take revenge. I keep it at fourteen-plus with Six of Crows: kisses only, but the violence is steady, a drug runs through the plot, and Inej's trafficking and Kaz's trauma are at the centre.",
    summary: "The Ice Court heist worked, and Kaz Brekker's crew was double-crossed. Inej has been kidnapped by the merchant Jan Van Eck, the drug jurda parem is loose in the world, and every power in Ketterdam wants the boy who knows how to make it. Kaz plans a con against the whole city: an auction, a fake saint, a rigged market and a set of reversals that turn on what each member of the crew is willing to give. Wylan confronts his father. Jesper's father arrives in town. Nina, recovering from parem, discovers what it has done to her power. Kaz confronts Pekka Rollins, the man who killed his brother. One of the Crows does not survive.",
    ripeFor: "Fourteen and up. Romance is kisses, and the most important romantic moment is Kaz and Inej trying to touch at all: Kaz's aversion to skin, after waking among the corpses of plague victims as a child, is handled with real care. Inej was trafficked into a brothel as a girl; the book is clear about that history without describing it. Violence is the band driver: knife fights, a torture threat with Inej's legs, Kaz's revenge, and the death of a main character. Nina's withdrawal from parem is written like an addiction. Language is mild. The Netflix series Shadow and Bone used the Crows in a different storyline; this book has not been adapted.",
    publisherNote: "Henry Holt (Macmillan), young adult. Content sits at the upper end of YA but within it.",
    seriesNote: "Book two of the duology. The King of Scars duology follows Nikolai and is similar in band.",
    sourcesNote: "Rated from the publisher record, trade reviews and consistent reader reporting.",
    sources: [{ label: "Leigh Bardugo: official site", url: "https://www.leighbardugo.com/" }],
    sig: [[1, "Kisses and the difficulty of touch."], [3, "Knife fights, torture threats, revenge, a main character's death."], [1, "Mild."], [3, "Withdrawal from an addictive magical drug, on the page."], [3, "Child trafficking in backstory, trauma, a brother's death, abusive fathers."]] }),
];
